Derived knowledge — where the CAN facts came from
-------------------------------------------------

Decoding a car's bus is a community effort, and Ha-Kake stands on other
people's published findings. Two projects in particular were used to
cross-check CAN arbitration IDs, byte offsets and scale factors while working
out the Leaf signals:

  Open Vehicle Monitoring System (OVMS), Nissan Leaf module
  `vehicle_nissanleaf.cpp` in Open-Vehicle-Monitoring-System-3
  https://github.com/openvehicles/Open-Vehicle-Monitoring-System-3
  License: MIT. Verified 2026-09-02 — the repository LICENSE file states
  "The following license covers the majority of code in this project: MIT
  License, Copyright (c) 2011-2017 Open Vehicles", and the header of
  `vehicle_nissanleaf.cpp` itself carries an MIT permission notice
  ((C) 2011 Michael Stegen / Stegen Electronics; (C) 2011-2017 Mark
  Webb-Johnson; (C) 2011 Sonny Chen @ EPRO/DX; (C) 2017 Tom Parker).
  Confidence: high for that file; the repository notes that some other files
  use different licenses.

  dalathegreat/leaf_can_bus_messages — DBC collection
  https://github.com/dalathegreat/leaf_can_bus_messages
  License: GNU General Public License v3. Verified 2026-09-02 from the
  repository's LICENSE file, which is the verbatim GPL-3.0 text.
  Confidence: high that GPL-3.0 is the stated repository license.

**No code from either project is present in Ha-Kake.** Every decoder here was
written from scratch against captures taken from the owner's own car, and every
signal was re-verified on that car before being marked verified in
`docs/SIGNALS.md`.

What was taken from those projects is *facts*: that a particular arbitration ID
carries a particular quantity, that a value sits at a given byte offset, that a
raw count scales by a given factor. Facts about how a physical device behaves
are not copyrightable, and observing that a Leaf reports pack voltage on a
certain CAN ID is a fact about the Leaf, not an expression owned by whoever
wrote it down first. The credit above is therefore offered as ordinary good
citizenship in the Leaf-hacking community — these people did the hard early
work in public and it saved real time — and not because a license required it.
Their licenses are recorded here so that anyone building on Ha-Kake can make
their own assessment rather than having to redo the research.

